# GitHub organization runner commands

The CLI uses the organization's GitHub App installation for runner management. These endpoints do not use repository administration or request new App permissions.

`runner-token --org <org>` deliberately bypasses the JSON envelope. Stdout carries only the registration token and a newline. Stderr carries the validated expiry or a sanitized failure. The command does not call attestation or write credentials to disk. Octokit logging is disabled for this client because request errors can carry credentials.

`runner-group --org <org> --name <name> --repo <owner/repo>` creates or restricts a group to exactly one private repository. It sets selected visibility and disables public repositories. Existing groups replace their repository allowlist. Readback verifies the effective policy before reporting success. The update uses separate policy and repository-access requests, so a failed second request can leave a partial update; rerun only after inspecting the reported endpoint failure. Dry-run reads the target and reports the intended policy without writes.

`runners --org <org> [--group <name>]` reads runners and their group membership. All collection reads follow GitHub pagination. A missing named group fails instead of silently returning an empty list.

## Registration expiry validation

GitHub's [registration-token example](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/actions/self-hosted-runners#create-a-registration-token-for-an-organization) includes fractional seconds and a timezone offset. The initial whole-second `Z`-only check rejected that valid response shape. A matching CLI fixture reproduced the failure without live credentials.

The expiry must be a string with an ISO timestamp shape, parse with `Date.parse` to a real instant, and be strictly later than `Date.now()`. Shape validation alone does not prove the expiry is valid or future. The token alphabet check remains unchanged. Invalid metadata fails with the fixed sanitized error and empty stdout.

CLI fixtures freeze the clock so offset, `Z`, expired, equal-to-now, malformed, and non-string expiry cases stay deterministic. They retain all credential-leak and subprocess assertions. No production token response belongs in the fixtures.

## Source and proof

- `packages/cli/src/org-runners.ts`: API operations and sanitized errors.
- `packages/cli/src/commands/runners.ts`: command adapters and token-only writer.
- `packages/cli/src/cli.ts`: command discovery and the token-only output exception.
- `packages/cli/test/runner-token.test.ts`: real CLI subprocesses with fake HTTP responses, temporary homes, and forbidden subprocess spawns.
- `packages/cli/test/org-runners.test.ts`: group policy, pagination, dry-run, permission failures, and runner membership.

These tests do not mint real tokens or mutate real runner groups. Live registration and deployment remain separate operator work.
